==== Flash Layout ====

**Stock flash layout** has 8 partitions:
  * ''0x00000000-0x00800000 : "ALL"''
  * ''0x00000000-0x00030000 : "Bootloader"''
  * ''0x00030000-0x00040000 : "Config"''
  * ''0x00040000-0x00050000 : "Factory"''
  * ''0x00050000-0x0019d3e0 : "Kernel"''
  * ''0x0019d3e0-0x00800000 : "RootFS"''
  * ''0x00050000-0x007f0000 : "Kernel_RootFS"''
  * ''0x007f0000-0x00800000 : "UserCfg"''

**OpenWrt flash layout** leaves the first 3 partitions and the last partition unchanged. Although they are renamed. Note the sizes of kernel, rootfs and rootfs_data are set based on the compiled kernel size so may change with new images. 
  * ''0x000000000000-0x000000030000 : "uboot"''
  * ''0x000000030000-0x000000040000 : "uboot-env"''
  * ''0x000000040000-0x000000050000 : "factory"''
  * ''0x000000050000-0x0000007f0000 : "firmware"''
  * ''0x000000000000-0x0000001657a4 : "kernel"''
  * ''0x0000001657a4-0x0000007a0000 : "rootfs"''
  * ''0x000000389000-0x0000007a0000 : "rootfs_data"''
  * ''0x0000007f0000-0x000000800000 : "user-cfg"''


==== OEM easy installation ====

<color #ed1c24>WARNING: The stock u-boot will fail to load a kernel after the kernel reaches a certain size. The snapshot builds are commonly that size.</color>

It is ideal to have a serial connection to verify u-boot loads the kernel, but if you don't have one the upgrade will just stall forever and you can reload the stock firmware from Option 1 below.

The snapshot builds as of 4/1/19 will fail and the kernel is over 1.6M. If you do a local build through the image builder using the release config.seed the kernel is smaller 1.5M and will load.

**Option 1 (from bootloader web)**

This is the easier option. It uses a tiny web server in the stock u-boot bootloader. So you are using u-boot to update flash directly without loading the stock firmware. This skips some of the oddities and validations that the full stock firmware applies on image updates.

  * Hold reset button on the back of router when plugging in power (for at-least 10 seconds after plugged in)
  * Connect to a Lan port
  * Set computer IP to 10.10.10.3
  * Go to http://10.10.10.123 in a web browser
  * Click the Browse… Button and select the *squashfs.sysupgrade.bin file then click APPLY

**Option 2 (from the stock admin web)**
  * Go to firmware upgrade
  * Upload the **factory** image *initramfs.bin first
  * Boot into openwrt
  * From Luci web in openwrt upload the *squashfs.sysupgrade.bin
==== OEM installation using the TFTP method ====

->  [[docs:guide-user:installation:generic.flashing.tftp]]

TFTP from stock is likely possible although has **not** been verified. The **//OEM easy installation - option 1//** above has been verified and does not require any TFTP software on your PC. TFTP from serial has been verified to work with the ip addresses below

^ Bootloader tftp server IPv4 address  | 10.10.10.3   |
^ TFTP client required IP address      | 10.10.10.123                                        |


